    {
      "id": "T4",
      "table_ref": "Table IV",
      "description": "Fama-MacBeth regressions of annual returns on the balance-sheet decomposition of asset growth into investment components (ΔCash, ΔCurAsst, ΔPPE, ΔOthAssets) and financing components (ΔRE, ΔStock, ΔDebt, ΔOpLiab).",
      "replication_meaning": "Demonstrates correct construction of the balance-sheet decomposition variables (investment side: Compustat #1 cash, #4-#1 noncash current assets, #8 PPE, residual other assets; financing side: #36 retained earnings, #130+#60+#38-#36 stock, #9+#34 debt, residual operating liabilities), each scaled by lagged total assets (#6 in t-2), and their use in Fama-MacBeth regressions. Matching the investment-component t-stats (ΔCurAsst and ΔPPE strongest) validates that the component-level asset-growth decomposition is built correctly, supporting the paper's mechanism that operating-asset growth drives the effect.",
      "paper_quote": "The investment decomposition defines total assets as the sum of: (1) Cash ($\\Delta Cash$: Compustat #1), (2) Noncash current assets ($\\Delta CurAsst$: Compustat #4 – Compustat #1), (3) Property, plant and equipment ($\\Delta PPE$: Compustat #8), and (4) Other assets ... Variables used in the cross-sectional regressions are changes in these variables from the fiscal year ending in calendar year $t-2$ to the fiscal year ending in calendar year $t-1$ scaled by total assets in the fiscal year ending in calendar year $t-2$. (L2678)",
      "exercises_preprocessing_rules": [
        "fm_tableIV_decomposition", "var_decomp_investment", "var_decomp_financing", "var_decomp_scaling",
